          'Cuteness may also shape how we pay attention. In three small experiments, Hiroshi Nittono and colleagues found that after viewing pictures of puppies and kittens, participants performed a fine-motor task and a visual search task more carefully than after viewing adult animals, and focused more narrowly. The samples were small and the tasks simple, so it would be a stretch to claim cute characters make anyone a better puzzler. The findings do help explain why a friendly face on a puzzle screen can feel steadying rather than distracting.',
