          'The study measured reactions to still images, not play, and correlations of this kind cannot prove cause. But it gives designers a vocabulary that is more useful than hue alone: the overall lightness and saturation of a scene, and how varied its colours are, may matter as much to its mood as which colours are in it. The authors use their results to propose “emotional colour scripting”, planning a game’s palette the way a film plans its lighting.',
